<section class="ph"><div class="ph-media"><img class="ph-img" src="/assets/img/photos/icicles.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/icicles-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/icicles.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="815" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap ph-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/michigan-claim-appraisal/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Ice Dam &amp; Frozen Pipe Appraisal</span></nav><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Appraisal service</p><h1>Ice Dam Claim Appraisal in Michigan for Winter Water Damage</h1><p class="ph-lede">Heavy lake-effect snow and deep cold make ice dams and frozen pipes a Michigan winter fixture. When an accepted claim is underpaid, we document the full scope.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-maize" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-glass" href="/request-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="ph-facts"><div><dt>Peak season</dt><dd>December through March</dd></div><div><dt>Common pushback</dt><dd>Maintenance and wear arguments</dd></div><div><dt>Hidden damage</dt><dd>Insulation and wall cavities</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Why Michigan winters produce so many ice dam claims</h2>
<p>Ice dams form when heat escaping through the roof melts snow, and the water refreezes at the colder eaves. The ridge of ice traps more meltwater, which backs up under shingles and into walls and ceilings. Michigan’s lake-effect snow belts, from the west Michigan lakeshore to the Keweenaw and Marquette areas, see heavy, persistent snowpack that makes the problem worse. So do older homes with minimal attic insulation, complex rooflines and recessed lights cut into top-floor ceilings.</p>
<p>Frozen pipes are the other half of the winter story. During the late-January 2019 polar vortex, extreme cold froze lines in exterior walls, crawlspaces and unheated garages across the state. When those pipes thaw and split, water can run for hours before anyone notices.</p>
<h2>The arguments insurers often raise</h2>
<h3>“It’s a maintenance issue”</h3>
<p>Ice dam claims are frequently met with wear-and-tear, maintenance or inadequate insulation arguments. Some policies contain specific language about ice dams, freezing or continuous seepage. Whether a loss is covered is not something appraisal decides, but where coverage is accepted, the scope and price of the repair are often disputed.</p>
<h3>“Only the visible stain”</h3>
<p>An estimate that paints a ceiling stain may ignore wet insulation, saturated drywall inside the wall cavity, swollen trim and hidden mold growth. Moisture readings and thermal imaging help show the true extent.</p>
<h3>Freezing limits for vacant or unheated homes</h3>
<p>Many policies limit freeze coverage if the home wasn’t heated or the water wasn’t shut off and drained. This matters for cottages and cabins up north that sit empty in winter. Read your policy’s conditions carefully, and if you own a seasonal property, check whether the insurer requires periodic inspections, a minimum thermostat setting or a drained plumbing system while it sits empty.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Coverage first, then amount</b>If your insurer has denied an ice dam or frozen pipe claim outright, appraisal isn’t the tool for that disagreement, so talk to a Michigan attorney. If the insurer has accepted the loss but underpaid it, appraisal may help.</div>
<h2>What a full scope often includes</h2>
<ul>
<li>removal and replacement of saturated drywall and insulation, not just the stained patch;</li>
<li>roof repairs at the eaves, including ice-and-water shield where code requires it;</li>
<li>interior trim, flooring and cabinetry affected by water;</li>
<li>drying equipment, monitoring and any mold remediation;</li>
<li>contents damage in the affected rooms.</li>
</ul>
<p>Matching is another frequent issue. A ceiling patched in one room may not blend with adjacent areas, and flooring that runs continuously through several rooms may need more than a single section replaced.</p>
<h2>Learn more and estimate your exposure</h2>
<p>Our <a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/ice-dam-claims-in-michigan/">ice dam claims guide</a> goes deeper on policy language and documentation. The <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/ice-dam-risk-cost-estimator/">ice dam risk and cost estimator</a> gives a rough sense of repair ranges. If you’re in the Upper Peninsula, our <a href="/mi-cities-served/marquette-insurance-appraiser/">Marquette page</a> covers local conditions. We are independent appraisers, not attorneys or public adjusters, and we don’t give legal advice.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary><span>Is ice dam damage covered by Michigan homeowner policies?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>It depends on your policy wording. Many policies cover sudden water damage from ice dams but exclude wear, maintenance or long-term seepage, so read your policy and ask your agent.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>My cottage pipes froze while it was empty. Can appraisal help?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Only if the insurer accepts coverage. Many policies limit freeze losses at unheated or unattended homes, and that coverage question is outside appraisal.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Why does the insurer’s estimate only cover the ceiling stain?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Initial estimates often miss water inside walls and insulation. Moisture readings and thermal imaging can document hidden damage for the appraisal panel.</p></div></details></div>
